// Reading an existing value at a **union** type argument where the declaration makes it unsound —
// refused at check time, one `E0007` per widening, exactly as the same widening to a `dyn Trait`,
// to the open `dyn` and to an abstract kind is refused in the sibling files.
//
// A union argument is a widening like the other three and is gated with them. Left open it corrupts
// a value the checker approved: a `Box<Dog>` read as a `Box<Dog | Cat>` takes a store of a `Cat`
// through the widened view, and the original — still declared `Box<Dog>` — answers `meow` to
// `speak`, or answers nothing at all where the member has no `speak`.
//
// Four refusals over the three occurrences that make a parameter unsafe to widen:
//
//   * `Box` is a reference `class` with a `mut` field of the parameter's type. A widened view of a
//     class IS the original, so the store lands in the original.
//   * A **superset** is the same widening between two unions: `Box<Dog | Cat>` read as a
//     `Box<Dog | Cat | Mouse>` takes a store of a `Mouse` the original has no arm for.
//   * `Matcher` takes the parameter as a **method parameter**, and `Pair` is a value `struct` that
//     does the same. Neither is about aliasing: the body of `matches` is checked believing `other`
//     is a `Dog`, so calling it through a widened receiver hands it a `Cat`.
//
// The reverse direction is a NARROWING and is refused wherever it appears, which the last function
// pins: `Box<Dog | Cat>` read as a `Box<Dog>` would hand out a `Cat` where the reader's own type
// says `Dog`. It carries no variance help, because no declaration would make it sound.
//
// Every widening sits in a function **nothing calls**: the refusal is static, so no program runs at
// all, and a runtime abort could not produce these.
// expect: error E0007 at 69:28
// expect: error E0007 at 74:36
// expect: error E0007 at 79:32
// expect: error E0007 at 84:29
// expect: error E0007 at 89:24
// expect: exit 1
struct Dog {
    fn speak(): string {
        return "woof"
    }
}

struct Cat {
    fn speak(): string {
        return "meow"
    }
}

struct Mouse {
    n: int
}

class Box<T> {
    pub mut v: T
}

class Matcher<T> {
    v: T

    pub fn matches(other: T): bool {
        return self.v == other
    }
}

struct Pair<T> {
    v: T

    pub fn matches(other: T): bool {
        return self.v == other
    }
}

fn widen_mut_field(b: Box<Dog>): string {
    wide: Box<Dog | Cat> = b
    return "unreachable"
}

fn widen_to_superset(b: Box<Dog | Cat>): string {
    wide: Box<Dog | Cat | Mouse> = b
    return "unreachable"
}

fn widen_method_param(m: Matcher<Dog>): string {
    wide: Matcher<Dog | Cat> = m
    return "unreachable"
}

fn widen_struct_method_param(p: Pair<Dog>): string {
    wide: Pair<Dog | Cat> = p
    return "unreachable"
}

fn narrow_out_of_union(b: Box<Dog | Cat>): string {
    narrow: Box<Dog> = b
    return "unreachable"
}
